  "text": "So it cuts latency, takes traffic off your origin, and absorbs spikes. Use it for anything static or cacheable: images, JavaScript, CSS and video. Dynamic, user-specific responses usually bypass it, or get a short time to live at the edge. A popular short link is the same redirect for everyone, so the edge can answer it too. The price: a click the edge answers never reaches your servers, so they can't count it, and a link you take down keeps working there until its time to live runs out. So keep that time short. For a read-heavy product with users everywhere, a CDN is the cheapest large win there is.\n\nThat's a first design. It's sized for its estimate, and no single machine can take it down. Now give it ten times the traffic. Next: scaling a design.",
